Output: {normalized_spec=Build a URL shortener service exposing REST APIs to create, retrieve, redirect, and delete shortened URLs, with support for custom aliases and optional expiration dates. The system must track analytics per shortened URL (e.g., click count, timestamps, referrer, geolocation, device/browser info) and expose an API to retrieve these metrics. Non-functional requirements include high availability, low-latency redirects (sub-100ms), horizontal scalability to handle high read/write volume, collision-resistant unique short code generation, and durability of stored mappings and analytics data., assumptions=[Short codes are auto-generated using a base62 encoding or similar scheme unless a custom alias is requested, Analytics data can be eventually consistent and does not need to block the redirect path, URLs do not expire by default unless an expiration is explicitly set, A relational or key-value datastore (e.g., DynamoDB, PostgreSQL) is acceptable for storing URL mappings, with a separate store or pipeline for analytics events, Authentication/authorization for API access is out of scope unless specified otherwise, Rate limiting is required to prevent abuse but exact thresholds are not specified], open_ambiguities=[What is the expected scale (requests per second, total URLs) the system must support?, Is user authentication/account management required for creating and managing URLs?, What level of analytics granularity is needed (real-time dashboards vs. batch reporting)?, Should short URLs support custom domains or only a single default domain?, What are the specific reliability targets (uptime SLA, disaster recovery requirements)?, Is there a requirement for malicious URL/spam detection before shortening?]}
