Output: {api_contract=POST /urls creates a shortened URL (accepts long URL, optional custom alias, optional expiration date; returns short code/URL). GET /urls/{code} retrieves metadata for a shortened URL (target, creation/expiration date, status). GET /{code} performs the actual redirect (HTTP 301/302 to the original URL), returning 404/410 if not found or expired. DELETE /urls/{code} deactivates/removes a mapping. GET /urls/{code}/analytics returns aggregated metrics (click count, timestamps, referrer, geolocation, device/browser breakdowns). All write endpoints are subject to rate limiting., data_model=UrlMapping: short_code (PK), long_url, custom_alias flag, created_at, expires_at (nullable), owner/creator (optional, unused until auth is scoped in), status (active/expired/deleted). AnalyticsEvent: event_id, short_code (FK), timestamp, referrer, geo_location, device_type, browser, ip_hash (for dedup, no auth-tied identity). AnalyticsSummary (derived/aggregated, eventually consistent): short_code, total_clicks, last_accessed, breakdowns by referrer/geo/device, updated periodically from raw events., short_code_strategy=Auto-generate short codes using a base62 encoding of a unique numeric ID (e.g., from a distributed counter or Snowflake-style ID generator) to ensure collision resistance without coordination overhead; fall back to a retry-on-collision check for edge cases. Custom aliases bypass generation but are validated for uniqueness against existing mappings before insertion. This approach supports horizontal scalability since ID generation can be sharded/distributed without a central bottleneck., caching_strategy=A read-through cache (e.g., Redis or CDN-edge cache) sits in front of the URL mapping store, caching short_code -> long_url lookups to meet the sub-100ms redirect latency requirement; cache entries respect TTL aligned with expiration dates and are invalidated on delete/update. Analytics writes are decoupled from the redirect path (e.g., async event emission to a queue/stream) so they never block or slow down the cached redirect response.}
